{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem is a standard unit-rate arithmetic exercise; its convention is that 'sells pencils at 7 for $2' states a unit price, so 21 pencils are uniquely determined to cost $6. SRC_001 misclassifies the problem as underdetermined by demanding an explicit covering principle the problem never requires, thereby refusing to answer the question as posed.","counterexample":[{"context":"standard school unit-rate word problem","statement":"sells pencils at 7 for $2","answer":"$6","determined":true}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 hedges the answer ($6) as a defeasible empirical expectation rather than a determined result. But the problem asks how much 21 pencils cost under the stated rate; with the standard unit-price interpretation the answer is determined as $6, not merely expected and observation-revisable. Treating it as an empirical prior conflates math convention with empirical uncertainty and fails to commit to the problem's requested value.","counterexample":[{"rate":"7 for $2","quantity":21,"computed":"$6","status":"determined"}],"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}